--- ## One job, driven from IXQL
--- A job is a declared service that runs to its end: `restart: never`, so once it exits the
--- plane leaves it `stopped`, its `last_exit` saying how, and does not start it again. Here the
--- job is `ix pipeline run` on a pipeline file: the real IX pipeline runner, under the plane.
---
---   ixctl up spec.json          -- a spec declaring `job` as
---                               --   ix pipeline run --file <ix.yaml> --json
---                               -- with `restart: never`
---   cargo run -p ix-ctl --example ixql-ctl -- crates/ix-ctl/examples/job.ixql --state <state file>
---
--- The runner prints every binding below as JSON.

desired <- {
    schema: "ixctl-desired/0",
    services: { "job": { state: "running" } }
  }

--- Answers the new generation at once; the start runs in the background.
applied <- desired → ix.ctl.apply

--- Converged means launched: the plane observed the start, not the end. A job's `desired`
--- stays `running` after it ends, so applying this document again would run it again.
started <- ix.ctl.wait(generation: applied.generation, epoch: applied.epoch, timeout_ms: 30000)

--- Answers the job's status entry once the run this generation started has exited: state
--- `stopped`, `last_exit.code` its exit code, `last_exit.generation` this generation. Without
--- `generation:` the last exit the plane saw would answer, an earlier run's included.
exited <- ix.ctl.wait(service: "job", exit: true, generation: applied.generation, epoch: applied.epoch, timeout_ms: 120000)

--- What the job printed. A stdout line that is not a `{kind, ...}` event reaches `svc/job` as a
--- `log` event carrying the line; `ix pipeline run --json` prints one `{"event": ...}` object per
--- line, `done` last. Replayed from the plane's ring, from the start of this boot.
output <- ix.ctl.events(channel: "svc/job", since: 0, epoch: applied.epoch, max: 100, timeout_ms: 1000)
